 "signs": [
  {
   "text": "THE PONY TRACK. Ben Nevis, Beinn Nibheis in Gaelic, 1,345 m: the highest mountain in the British Isles. A weather observatory stood on top from 1883 to 1904. Visibility was often nil. The tea was good.",
   "x": 15.0,
   "y": 12.0
  },
  {
   "text": "LOCH LINNHE. A long sea loch running in from the Atlantic to the foot of Ben Nevis, with seals, otters and the odd porpoise. Fort William's Gaelic name is An Gearasdan: 'the Garrison'.",
   "x": 14.0,
   "y": 34.0
  },
  {
   "text": "THE HIGH STREET. Pedestrians only, with pubs, outdoor shops and a hundred ways to buy a mug with a stag on it. It was once next to a fort, named for King William III, long gone. The street's still here. The mugs have multiplied.",
   "x": 33.0,
   "y": 22.0
  },
  {
   "text": "THE WEST HIGHLAND LINE. Moors, lochs and mountains all the way to Mallaig. The Jacobite steam train, the one filmed on the Glenfinnan Viaduct, starts right here. The ticket office does not do dragons.",
   "x": 35.0,
   "y": 29.0
  },
  {
   "x": 32,
   "y": 19,
   "text": "BLUE PLAQUE: Weather was recorded on the summit of Ben Nevis every hour from 1883 to 1904.",
   "kind": "plaque",
   "dx": -0.475,
   "plaque": "fort_william_observatory",
   "title": "Ben Nevis Observatory"
  },
  {
   "x": 34,
   "y": 20,
   "plaque": "fort_william_secret_portrait",
   "title": "The Secret Portrait",
   "text": "The West Highland Museum keeps a secret portrait of Bonnie Prince Charlie. Lying flat it is just a smear of colour, but stand a polished metal cylinder on it and his face appears in the reflection. After 1746 that was a handy way to toast a prince without getting arrested."
  },
  {
   "x": 15,
   "y": 28,
   "plaque": "fort_william_inverlochy",
   "title": "Inverlochy Castle",
   "text": "Just north of town stands old Inverlochy Castle, a square stone fort built by the Comyns in the 1200s. In February 1645 the Marquis of Montrose marched his army over the snowy hills and routed the Campbells beside it. The old walls are still standing, and still look slightly smug."
  },
  {
   "x": 44,
   "y": 22,
   "plaque": "fort_william_smelter",
   "title": "The Aluminium Smelter",
   "text": "Since 1929 an aluminium smelter has run at Lochaber, just outside town. It is powered by water from Loch Treig, sent through a tunnel about 15 miles long, bored under the Ben Nevis range. Somewhere under your boots, a mountain is quietly making electricity."
  },
  {
   "x": 41,
   "y": 33,
   "plaque": "fort_william_west_highland_way",
   "title": "West Highland Way",
   "text": "The West Highland Way, Scotland's first official long-distance route, opened in 1980. It runs 96 miles from Milngavie, near Glasgow, and finishes here in Fort William. Walkers arrive limping, sunburnt and bitten, and immediately ask where the chip shop is."
  }
 ],
